		{"id":"schemas","type":"text","text":"### Inside EVERY database: one schema per temple — `t_noida` · `t_gnw` · `t_nex` · `t_whf` · …\nNamed identically in silo and pool, so promoting a temple to its own shard is a **registry row change plus a schema move**.\nPostgreSQL cannot join or FK across databases **or** across hosts — so service data ownership and temple separation are enforced by structure, not by discipline.\n\n⚠️ ~240 tables per temple per service database drives the **~50-temple ceiling per shard** — every extra service database lowers it.","x":880,"y":750,"width":1140,"height":130,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"notes","type":"text","text":"# Decisions locked\n\n**Orchestration — k3s, self-hosted** (same class as Forgejo/Jenkins/Vikunja). $0 control plane. Kubernetes from day one, so there is no future migration; EKS later = pointing `kubectl` elsewhere.\n\n**Data — Model D (bridge), built D-minimal.** Two shards in every environment from day one; dev/UAT use Postgres containers. Prod day-1: `shard-noida` (silo) + `shard-pool-1` (GNW, NEX, WHF).\n\n**Stack** Java 25 LTS · Maven · Spring Data JPA (CRUD) + jOOQ (reporting) · JobRunr\n\n**Front door** Traefik for TLS and host routing; Spring Cloud Gateway behind it for JWT, temple resolution and rate limits. Auth sits behind the gateway — a login bug must not take donations down.\n\n**Comms** REST for synchronous answers + **RabbitMQ** for announcements, published via transactional outbox.\n\n**Cross-temple screens: NONE.** Every screen is one temple. No data warehouse. Verified: today's PHP app has zero `DB::connection()` calls and one set of DB credentials — it already cannot read another temple.\n\n**Audit & activity logs** stay inside the temple's own schema. Audit is a library, not a service.\n\n**MySQL → PostgreSQL: big-bang, temple by temple.** Order: UAT → GNW/NEX → WHF → **Noida last**. ~2.95 GB total converts in minutes. MySQL kept read-only as the rollback until a clean reconciliation cycle passes.\n\n**Team: solo (+ Claude), no fixed deadline.** Phasing, not parallelism.\n\n---\n\n# The two irreversible precautions\n🔴 **No cross-temple SQL**\n🔴 **No cross-temple foreign keys**\nTwo shards make both physically impossible — structure instead of discipline.\n\n# k3s operating rules\n1. **All manifests in git** — the cluster is reconstructible from the repo; the k3s datastore is then a convenience, not a dependency\n2. **Upgrade k3s once a year** — the same restart renews its internal TLS certificates\n3. **AWS permissions: start with a narrow shared node role; move to OIDC-based per-service roles (IRSA-style) before the first temple goes live.** Never long-lived access keys — the Jenkinsfile's \"no AWS keys anywhere\" rule stands\n4. ECR pulls need a containerd registry credential helper or a token-refresh CronJob\n\n# Supporting precautions\n🟠 Registry resolves temple → host; no hard-coded DB URLs\n🟠 Datasources lazy, cached, refreshable without restart\n🟠 Migration fan-out driven by the registry, grouped by shard\n🟠 Temple context on every service hop and every async job\n🟡 Database names constant across shards; only host varies\n🟡 Uniform schema naming `t_<temple>`\n🟡 `SET LOCAL`, never session `SET`\n🟡 Per-schema dump/restore tooling, tested\n🟡 Pool sizes configured per shard\n🟡 Registry cache TTL + invalidation\n\n---\n\n# Still open\n- RabbitMQ in-cluster vs Amazon MQ\n- GitOps tool: ArgoCD vs Flux vs plain `kubectl apply` from Jenkins\n- Single node vs HA pair · x86 vs Graviton\n- Time Management app — parked until the platform is settled","x":2200,"y":-990,"width":440,"height":800,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"runtime","type":"text","text":"## Runtime: k3s\nSelf-hosted Kubernetes on EC2 — one binary, $0 control plane.\n\nGives: self-healing · health-based replacement · **zero-downtime rolling deploys** · CronJobs · Ingress + cert-manager.\n\nCosts: ~600–700 MB for the control plane · yours to upgrade annually · per-service IAM needs OIDC setup (rule 3).","x":2200,"y":-170,"width":440,"height":230,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"ai","type":"text","text":"### AI Agent\nPython · FastAPI\ncalls platform APIs **through the gateway**","x":2200,"y":80,"width":440,"height":130,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"obs","type":"text","text":"### Observability\nOpenTelemetry — traces · metrics · logs\n**every span tagged `tenant_id`**\nThis is where all-temple questions get answered","x":2200,"y":230,"width":440,"height":170},
		{"id":"cicd","type":"text","text":"### Delivery\nForgejo → Jenkins → ECR → **k3s**\none pipeline template per service\nregistry-driven migration fan-out\ncanary + rollout rings","x":2200,"y":420,"width":440,"height":180},
		{"id":"legend","type":"text","text":"**v3 — target platform architecture: Model D data layer on a self-hosted k3s cluster.**\nSupersedes `target-architecture-v2-100-tenants.canvas`, which assumed database-per-temple sharded. v1 (`target-architecture-as-stated.canvas`) and v2 are kept as history.\nSee `multi-tenancy-model-comparison.canvas` for why data models A, B and C were rejected.\n\n_Updated 2026-09-26 — orchestration, broker, cutover style and IAM path resolved · Time Management deliberately parked_","x":100,"y":980,"width":1100,"height":200}
	],
